<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  AGENT-NOTE: macOS drops kern.maxvnodes/kern.maxfiles/kern.maxfilesperproc on every reboot, and a
  process cannot raise them for itself. This one-shot root daemon re-applies the ceilings at boot.
  Values are the single source of truth: `just prep-kernel-limits` verifies the live sysctls against
  this ProgramArguments list, so edit them here only.

  Why vnodes dominate: each Lean process mmaps its whole artifact closure (5 vnodes per module:
  .olean, .olean.private, .olean.server, .ir, .ir.sig) and the `lake` parent maps the same closure
  again, so one `lake env lean <driver>` holds ~20-40k of the pool for its lifetime; when the pool
  empties XNU returns ENFILE system-wide (every process, pi/mise/shell included).

  Second ceiling, different errno: launchd's per-process fd default ships as `maxfiles 256
  unlimited`, which every launchd-spawned agent inherits and dies on at 256 fds (EMFILE, errno 24 -
  not the ENFILE above). The hard limit is left at `unlimited` because SIP rejects changing it at
  all: per `man launchctl` there is no soft-only form (3 args sets both), so every attempt touches the
  hard limit and always fails with "150 Operation not permitted while System Integrity Protection is
  engaged". The soft limit is applied before that failure, and the soft limit is what processes
  inherit, so stderr on these two calls is discarded to keep the log readable - `just
  audit-kernel-limits` verifies the resulting soft limit, which is the real check.
  Both ceilings live in this one ProgramArguments, so this file stays the single
  source of truth for `just prep-kernel-limits` and `just audit-kernel-limits`.

  Deliberately NOT managed: kern.maxfiles. macOS rests it at INT_MAX (2147483647) or thereabouts, so
  any explicit value here can only *lower* the ceiling - and the file table was never the binding
  constraint anyway (peak observed during a 1,215-event vnode storm: 8,385 of 368,640). The vnode
  pool above is what actually fills, so maxfiles is left to the kernel. If a future workload ever
  makes the file table the constraint, manage it here *and* check the resting default first.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>org.infra.kernel-limits</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-c</string>
		<string>/usr/sbin/sysctl -w kern.maxvnodes=524288 kern.maxfilesperproc=262144; /bin/launchctl limit maxfiles 256 unlimited 2>/dev/null; /bin/launchctl limit maxfiles 65536 unlimited 2>/dev/null</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<false/>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/org.infra.kernel-limits.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/org.infra.kernel-limits.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
